The hard-boundary callback now runs once after each completed constrained-MD
step; rejected steps restore force bookkeeping and reflected steps are
re-projected and force-re-evaluated. Rejected Langevin proposals retain their
consumed random draws so retries use fresh noise. Reflected LAMMPS integration
refreshes forces at the callback-adjusted position, while rejected integration
refreshes forces, energy, and virial data at the restored position. Callback
state is owned by each session and fix instance, so independent sessions can
set, clear, and destroy callbacks concurrently.
